{
  "example": "For a function preceded by a doc comment, an attribute, and an attached citation comment, a Rust-item input starts at the earliest attached line and ends at the function's final line. Its `text` contains those complete raw source lines. Inserting lines before them changes the displayed range without changing the input. {cite}`requirements.ci.rust_item_inputs`",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "The one test referencing this example covers the first half of the scenario but not the second.\n\n**What is covered.** `tests/commands.rs::add_input_modes_capture_raw_sources_and_refresh_idempotently` (about line 780) carries the `{test}` reference. It builds `src/main.rs` with these lines:\n- `/// user docs`\n- `#[allow(dead_code)]`\n- a `{impl}` citation comment\n- `fn main() { fn nested() {} }`\n\nIt then adds a Rust-item input with `--rust-item src/main.rs:4`. The assertions show:\n- The stored `text` is exactly the four complete raw lines, from the doc comment through the function's last line (`requirement[\"inputs\"][1][\"text\"]`).\n- `info` displays the range as `src/main.rs:1–4`.\n- Re-adding the same input reports \"unchanged\" and leaves the evidence file byte-identical.\n\nThis matches the first two sentences of the example: the range starts at the earliest attached line and ends at the function's last line, and `text` holds those raw lines.\n\n**What is not covered.** The example's last sentence says \"Inserting lines before them changes the displayed range without changing the input.\" That sentence also reflects the requirement in `requirements.ci.rust_item_inputs` that moving unchanged lines must not invalidate the input. No test does this:\n- The referenced test never edits `src/main.rs` after capture, so the line range never moves.\n- It never re-validates an input against a shifted file.\n- It never checks that the displayed range changes while the stored input stays the same.\n\nI found no other test that exercises this. The other Rust-item tests (`add_input_external_sources_respect_allow_and_disallow` and the cases in `tests/evidence_boundaries.rs`) use single-line or unshifted sources. The only test referencing the example therefore leaves the example's insertion/shift outcome unverified.\n\nI did not review the implementation in `src/rust_items.rs` and `src/evidence.rs` in detail. It may well handle the shift correctly, but the example is not demonstrated by a test, and I found no FIXME acknowledging the gap.",
